.TH mbglb2tiles 1 "7 November 2026"
.SH NAME
mbglb2tiles \- make an OGC 3D Tiles tileset of levels of detail from a GLB mesh of mbmesh or mbgrd2gltf
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmbglb2tiles\fR \fB-I\fI mesh.glb\fR [\fB-O\fI outputdir\fR]
[\fB--tile-size\fI meters\fR] [\fB--weld-tolerance\fI meters\fR] [\fB--threads\fI n\fR]
[\fB--no-draco\fR] [\fB--draco-precision\fI meters\fR] [\fB--draco-normal-bits\fI n\fR]
[\fB--html\fR [\fB--x3dom-url\fI url\fR] [\fB--sse\fI pixels\fR]] [\fB--lift\fR[=\fImeters\fR]] [\fB-V\fR] [\fB-h\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
\fBmbglb2tiles\fR turns a triangle mesh in a binary glTF (GLB) file written by \fBmbmesh\fR or
\fBmbgrd2gltf\fR into an OGC 3D Tiles tileset that a viewer loads a part at a time, at a level of detail
that depends on the distance. A mesh of tens of millions of vertices, which a browser cannot decode and
hold at once, is shown as the few tiles that are near enough to need their detail and coarser tiles for the rest.
Nothing is reconstructed: the mesh is read, coarsened, and cut into tiles.
.LP
The GLB must carry the georeference that \fBmbmesh\fR and \fBmbgrd2gltf\fR write in the extras of the
asset (\fIgeoreference\fR, with the vertices either absolute WGS84 ECEF coordinates or relative to a center),
because that says where the mesh is on the Earth. Any other GLB is refused. The mesh may have many primitives, as the GLB of
\fBmbgrd2gltf\fR and, by default, the \fImesh.glb\fR of \fBmbmesh\fR have, Draco-compressed
(KHR_draco_mesh_compression) or not. A GLB whose vertices are absolute ECEF coordinates in 32-bit floats resolves only
about 0.5 m, and the program warns; make the mesh with a GeoOrigin (\fB--geoorigin\fR of \fBmbmesh\fR or \fBmbgrd2gltf\fR).
.LP
The program works as follows.
.RS
.TP
.B Weld
The primitives of a GLB each have their own copies of the vertices on their edges, each quantized on its own. Vertices closer than
the weld tolerance are made one, and the normals are made again from the whole surface, so the shading is smooth across the
edges of primitives and tiles.
.TP
.B Frame
The mesh is put in the east-north-up frame at the middle of its bounds. The tiles are in that frame, written Y-up as glTF requires, and the
transform of the root tile of \fItileset.json\fR places it on the Earth.
.TP
.B Levels
The finest level is the mesh. Each level above clusters the vertices of the mesh in cubes whose side is the spacing of the mesh
times two to the power of the level, each cluster becoming the mean of its vertices, and drops the triangles that collapse or repeat. The
spacing is the square root of the area of the triangles seen from above divided by the number of vertices. The whole mesh is
coarsened and then cut, so that the tiles of a level meet exactly along their edges.
.TP
.B Tiles
The tiles of a level are the squares of a lattice, anchored at a corner of the bounds of the mesh, whose side is the tile size times
two to the power of the level, and each triangle goes to the tile that has its center. Four tiles of a level are replaced by one of the next level,
until one tile holds everything, so every tile has about as many vertices as any other. A tile is a GLB of one primitive, compressed with Draco
unless \fB--no-draco\fR is given.
.TP
.B tileset.json
Version 1.1, with the refinement REPLACE, a box bounding volume for each tile that holds its content and its children, and a geometric error
that is the cube size of its level (0 for the finest tiles). The tileset has the georeference and provenance of the run in its extras, as the tiles do in
the extras of their assets.
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-I\fI mesh.glb\fR, \fB--input\fR \fImesh.glb\fR
The GLB mesh. Required.
.TP
.B \-O\fI outputdir\fR, \fB--output\fR \fIoutputdir\fR
Where \fItileset.json\fR and the tiles go. The directory is created if needed. The tiles are in a directory \fIL0\fR,
\fIL1\fR and so on for each level, \fIL0\fR being the finest, named \fItile_I_J.glb\fR after the position of the tile in the lattice of its level.
The default is \fItileset\fR in the directory of the input, which is where \fBmbmesh --tileset\fR writes its tileset, in the same layout. A tileset
already in the directory, from either program, is replaced: its \fItileset.json\fR, \fIL\fRlevel directories and flat \fItile_I_J.glb\fR files are
removed first, and the log says so.
.TP
.B \--tile-size\fI meters\fR
The side of the tiles of the finest level. The default is 256 times the spacing of the mesh, to two significant digits, as
\fBmbgrd2gltf\fR writes blocks of 256 cells. The side doubles with each level.
.TP
.B \--weld-tolerance\fI meters\fR
Vertices of the file this close to each other are one. The default is twice the Draco quantization step that the file records, or 0.001 if that is
less.
.TP
.B \--threads\fI n\fR
The number of threads that decode the Draco primitives of the input and write the tiles. The default is the number of cores, at most 8.
.TP
.B \--no-draco
Write the tiles as arrays, without compression. By default they are compressed with Draco.
.TP
.B \--draco-precision\fI meters\fR
With Draco, keep positions to this many meters (default 0.001): each tile gets the bits that make the step of its quantization no more than
this over its extent. The same option as \fBmbmesh\fR and \fBmbgrd2gltf\fR take.
.TP
.B \--draco-normal-bits\fI n\fR
With Draco, the bits of each component of a normal, 2 to 30 (default 10).
.TP
.B \--html
Also write a viewer page, \fIoutputdir\fR.html in the directory that holds the output directory, which shows the tileset with an X3DOM GeoTileset node, with a
GeoOrigin at the origin of the frame and an overview viewpoint from above. The processing log is in \fImbglb2tiles.log\fR in the output
directory, which the page loads when its log panel is opened. The page must be served over HTTP, as it loads the tiles; the program prints
the command that serves it (\fBpython3 -m http.server\fR), and does not start it.
.TP
.B \--x3dom-url\fI url\fR
The X3DOM script that the \fB--html\fR page loads (default https://www.x3dom.org/x3dom/dist/x3dom-full.js). A GeoTileset needs a build of X3DOM
that has it, which that one may not.
.TP
.B \--lift\fR[=\fImeters\fR]
Put the tileset above the ellipsoid, along the local vertical, in the transform of \fBtileset.json\fR. As \fB--geoorigin\fR of \fBmbmesh\fR does, without the option there is no lift and the tileset has the true heights (a warning says so if any are below the ellipsoid), the option alone takes the default below, and a value must be attached, as in \fB--lift=900\fR. X3DOM's GeoTileset takes the
height of the camera as at least 1 m, so a camera below the ellipsoid, as over the seafloor, never gets close enough to refine a tile and only the root tile
loads. The default lift is enough to put the lowest point of the mesh 10 m above the ellipsoid, to a meter, if it is lower. The lift is logged
and written as \fBliftMeters\fR in the extras of the tileset, and the viewpoints of the \fB--html\fR page have it added.
.TP
.B \--sse\fI pixels\fR
With \fB--html\fR, the maximumScreenSpaceError of the page's GeoTileset: a tile is replaced by its children when its geometric error would show larger than
this (default 1; X3DOM's own default of 8 refines tiles of centimeter errors only within a few meters).
.TP
.B \-V\fR, \fB--verbose\fR
Log the debug messages as well as the information messages. The log always says what each stage does and which options are in effect, default or not.
.TP
.B \-h\fR, \fB--help\fR
Print the usage and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.LP
Make the tiles of a mesh from \fBmbmesh\fR, and a page to look at them with:
.br
mbmesh -I datalist.mb-1 -L 0.1 --geoorigin
.br
mbglb2tiles -I mbmesh_output/mesh.glb --html
.br
python3 -m http.server 8000 --directory .
.LP
Tiles of 50 m at the finest level, not compressed:
.br
mbglb2tiles -I ZTopo.glb --tile-size 50 --no-draco
.SH NOTES
.LP
The whole mesh is held in memory, about 100 bytes for each vertex while it is welded and clustered; a mesh of 38 million vertices
needed 12 GB. The tiles are in one frame for the whole tileset, which is fine for a survey a few kilometers across. The coarse levels are made by
clustering vertices, not by collapsing edges with an error measure, so thin features and sharp edges round off. \fB3d-tiles-validator\fR
reports no errors or warnings for the tilesets, only that it cannot validate the Draco extension.
